WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.268
Plans are useful because the future is uncertain, not because the future is perfectly knowable.

2
00:00:06.268 --> 00:00:10.060
Estimates are guesses made with incomplete information.

3
00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:26.001
The planning fallacy is the tendency to underestimate how long work will take, especially when the plan imagines clean execution and ignores interruptions, dependencies, rework, decision delays, maintenance, and administrative overhead.

4
00:00:26.001 --> 00:00:29.096
Good planning treats uncertainty as expected.

5
00:00:29.096 --> 00:00:31.804
Buffer time is not laziness or pessimism.

6
00:00:31.804 --> 00:00:33.661
It is a way to absorb reality.

7
00:00:33.661 --> 00:00:41.013
Dependencies slip, systems fail, approvals take longer than expected, and people need time to switch contexts.

8
00:00:41.013 --> 00:00:48.055
Unknown unknowns appear most often in work that is new, cross-functional, technical, or high consequence.

9
00:00:48.055 --> 00:00:51.846
A plan with no buffer may look efficient, but it is fragile.

10
00:00:51.846 --> 00:00:55.174
A single surprise can collapse the whole sequence.

11
00:00:55.174 --> 00:00:57.960
Maintenance work deserves explicit time.

12
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:06.859
In teams, this includes documentation, cleanup, access reviews, backlog grooming, patching, handoffs, and post-incident learning.

13
00:01:06.859 --> 00:01:13.127
In personal life, it includes chores, scheduling, finance, errands, and relationship upkeep.

14
00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:17.073
Maintenance often lacks urgency until ignored for too long.

15
00:01:17.073 --> 00:01:26.050
When it is invisible, people overcommit because they plan only the headline tasks and not the work that keeps life and operations running.

16
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.448
Useful plans include review points.

17
00:01:28.448 --> 00:01:34.871
A review point asks what changed, what still matters, what is blocked, and what should be adjusted.

18
00:01:34.871 --> 00:01:38.586
Adjusting a plan is not the same as abandoning direction.

19
00:01:38.586 --> 00:01:42.455
It is how direction survives contact with real conditions.

20
00:01:42.455 --> 00:01:51.122
The plan should act like a map: helpful enough to guide movement, flexible enough to revise when the terrain turns out to be different.
